S={}
S['google-io']=('s-b-io-hero','Bundles: the game that unlocked the I/O 2026 date','''[
      {k:"(The Brief)", h:"Make the date reveal something people earn together.",
       p:["Every year, developers watch for the Google I/O date drop. For 2026, Left Field Labs and Google wanted the reveal to feel like an event of its own instead of a press release.",
          "The idea was simple to say and hard to build: bundles of small experimental games, where every round played by anyone in the world pushed a shared counter toward unlocking the official date."]},
      {k:"(Direction)", h:"Google’s brand, treated like a physical material.",
       p:["I led art direction and design. We treated Google’s palette as folded, bent and layered cards with light moving across them, so each bundle had its own personality and still read instantly as Google. Finish a bundle and its card flips, folds and resolves into the Gemini spark.",
          "Type stayed calm and huge so the play could be loud. “Let’s play” did most of the talking."]},
      {media:[["s-b-io-direction","The layered-card language: the bundle shelf, a card mid-flip and the Gemini spark","full"]]},
      {k:"(The Games)", h:"One visual language across every game.",
       p:["With many games in flight at once, the look could drift fast. I set the art direction as a system: the card language, color and type rules, and how every win, badge and progress state looks, so Emoji Code, Sketch Race, Sound in Motion and Color Sense each felt new and still read as one experience."]},
      {media:[["s-b-io-games","Emoji Code, Sketch Race, Sound in Motion and Color Sense","full"]]},
      {k:"(On Every Screen)", h:"Built for the phone in your pocket.",
       p:["Most people played on their phones, between sessions and on the way to work. Every game was designed for touch first, and we prototyped in the browser with engineering from the start, so what got approved in review is what shipped."]},
      {media:[["s-b-io-mobile","The full loop on mobile, from Let’s Play to the date","full"]]},
      {k:"(The Reveal)", h:"Everyone played. Everyone unlocked it.",
       p:["Every play anywhere filled one shared bar. When it filled, the date became a build of its own: May 19–20, rendered in the same layered cards, ready to explore and share."]},
      {media:[["s-b-io-reveal","Community progress filling, then the date drop","full"]]},
      {k:"(The Payoff)", h:"Something to show for it.",
       p:["Players who finished every bundle got badges to post, a hidden remix to unlock and an invitation to make a build their own. Daily reviews with Left Field Labs and weekly check-ins with Google kept every bundle on brand and on schedule through launch."]},
      {media:[["s-b-io-payoff","You did it: badges, the hidden unlock and remix","full"]]},
    ]''')
S['pga-tour']=('s-b-pga-hero','YourTOUR, the live leaderboard and hole detail','''[
      {k:"(The Brief)", h:"A powerful app that made fans do all the work.",
       p:["At Shockoe, we partnered with the PGA Tour on its flagship app. It had scores, stats, video and news, organized the way the Tour thinks about golf: by event and by round. Fans think about golf by player. Checking on your favorite golfer took several taps and a lot of scrolling.",
          "The brief was onboarding, personalization and a simpler core experience, with more of the Tour’s energy in the product."]},
      {k:"(Onboarding)", h:"Pick your players first. Everything else follows.",
       p:["Favorites moved to the very start. Pick your tours and your players, and those choices shape the home feed, the alerts and what surfaces during a live round. That became YourTOUR, a feed that leads with the story of the day for the players you care about."]},
      {media:[["s-b-pga-onboard","Choose your tours, set the scene, land in YourTOUR, keep your favorites","full"]]},
      {k:"(Live Golf)", h:"Shot-level data, readable at a glance.",
       p:["Golf produces a huge amount of data. We kept what a fan actually checks, laid over the course itself: the leaderboard, the group on the tee, par, yardage and scoring on every hole, and TourCast tracing every shot."]},
      {media:[["s-b-pga-live","Leaderboard, group scorecard, hole detail, TourCast and the full course","full"]]},
      {k:"(Coverage)", h:"One number that matters, big.",
       p:["News, player profiles, stats and Watch got the same treatment: the number or the story that matters most, large, with the detail a tap away. Every live stream sits one tap from the leaderboard."]},
      {media:[["s-b-pga-coverage","Explore, a player profile, tournament stats and Watch","full"]]},
      {k:"(Stories)", h:"The round, told like a highlight reel.",
       p:["Between shots, the app tells the story of the day. Full-screen stories open on the round and close on the champion, and every tournament gets a hub with its own course photography.",
          "Every screen was built on the Haskell design system, which has its own case study."]},
      {media:[["s-b-pga-stories","Stories for the round and the champion, and the tournament hubs","full"]]},
    ]''')
S['haskell']=('s-b-hk-hero','Haskell: the system behind every PGA Tour screen','''[
      {k:"(The Brief)", h:"A digital evolution, built on one foundation.",
       p:["The PGA Tour was reimagining its digital experience: every shot live, every hole in 3D, and stories that follow the players fans care about. Ambitions that big need a system underneath, or every new feature turns into a one-off.",
          "Haskell is that system. I helped define its foundations and components and carried them into the product, so the vision work and the shipping app spoke the same language."]},
      {k:"(Principles)", h:"Fan focused. Immersive.",
       p:["Every decision traced back to two principles: put the players fans follow front and center, and bring them directly into the competition with live scores, short video and the course itself."]},
      {media:[["s-b-hk-principles","Principles: Fan Focused and Immersive","full"]]},
      {k:"(Foundations)", h:"Tokens named for their job.",
       p:["Suisse Int’l brings clarity and Tobias brings character, across a scale that runs from hero moments down to dense stats. Underneath sit the tokens for color, motion and space, each named for what it does rather than its value, on a 4dp grid.",
          "Motion got the same care as color. One easing curve drives every transition, and a linear curve handles anything that scales or scrubs."]},
      {media:[["s-b-hk-found","Motion durations, spacing variables, spacing methods, radii and easing","full"]]},
      {k:"(Specs)", h:"Rules a partner can follow without a meeting.",
       p:["Every part of the system has a spec: logos at 600 by 600 with room for the mask, tab bar icons in a 24dp grid with 1.5 to 2dp strokes, and colors that hold a 4.5:1 contrast ratio."]},
      {media:[["s-b-hk-specs","Color, tab bar icon and logo specs","full"]]},
      {k:"(Themes)", h:"Big events, without forking the system.",
       p:["Tournaments like The Players get their own palette and imagery. The theme swaps colors and art, and every component underneath stays the same, from the tournament hub to the leaderboard."]},
      {media:[["s-b-hk-themes","Theme palette, tournament themes and themed screens","full"]]},
      {k:"(Art Direction)", h:"Rules for the course itself.",
       p:["Golf is as much about place as players, so Haskell covers imagery too: aerial vistas, top-down flyovers and golden-hour light, with specs for frame rate and stabilization. Design and engineering now share one source of truth, and the same patterns run from the leaderboard to TourCast shot tracking."]},
      {media:[["s-b-hk-art","Moodboard, top-down, aerial and TourCast","full"]]},
    ]''')
S['rock-hall']=('s-b-rh-hero','The Rolling Stones, inducted 1989','''[
      {k:"(The Brief)", h:"Put the museum on every screen.",
       p:["With Grow, we partnered with the Rock &amp; Roll Hall of Fame on its digital experience. The museum is full of guitars, stage clothes, posters and records. Online, the site was mostly tickets, hours and a list of names.",
          "Our idea was to treat every inductee page like a gallery. I worked on art direction, UX and UI, and led visual direction on the inductee pages, where the objects, the music and the story come together. Here is one inductee, the Rolling Stones, followed down the page."]},
      {k:"(Artifacts)", h:"Every object tells part of the story.",
       p:["First comes an object from the collection, lit like it sits behind glass, with the story beside it, a button to view it in 3D and footage below. A black Les Paul, a concert poster: the same module carries any object, and the layout bends to fit it."]},
      {media:[["s-b-rh-artifacts","A black Les Paul in 3D, and a Stones concert poster from the collection","full"]]},
      {k:"(Their Sound)", h:"Pull a record and it plays.",
       p:["Records work like the objects. Pull one from the stack and it slides out of its sleeve and plays. Photo spreads from the era sit beside the music, so the page sounds and looks like the band."]},
      {media:[["s-b-rh-sound","Their signature sound, and the most satisfying hook of all time","full"]]},
      {k:"(Their Voice)", h:"Let the artists say it.",
       p:["Backstage access puts interviews right on the page, so Mick and Keith tell the story themselves. Pull quotes run big and plain, in the Hall’s yellow, and the induction essay sits one tap away."]},
      {media:[["s-b-rh-voice","Backstage with Mick and Keith, the 1989 essay and a pull quote","full"]]},
      {k:"(The Archive)", h:"One visit turns into an hour.",
       p:["Influence maps connect each inductee to the artists who shaped them and the ones they shaped, and a timeline walks through the career, so every page leads somewhere else in the Hall."]},
      {media:[["s-b-rh-archive","Their influence, and a timeline from Ed Sullivan to the Tour of the Americas","full"]]},
      {k:"(The System)", h:"One library runs all of it.",
       p:["The same component library carries the whole site, from inductee heroes and feature blocks to class listings, ticketing and the live induction broadcast."]},
      {media:[["s-b-rh-system","Heroes, feature blocks, class listings and calls to action from the library","full"]]},
    ]''')
S['dickies']=('s-b-dk-hero','Dickies.com: home, product, category and Skateboarding','''[
      {k:"(The Brief)", h:"A site as hardworking as the people who shop it.",
       p:["Dickies has made workwear since 1922. Its customers are welders, ranchers, skaters and chefs who are hard on their clothes and have no patience for a fussy website.",
          "Working with VF Corporation and the Dickies brand team, we redesigned the site as part of a wider brand refresh with a new identity and a custom typeface drawn from the logo. I led UI and visual design, carrying that refresh into a responsive store that balances grit with utility."]},
      {k:"(Shopping)", h:"Product in front of people, sooner.",
       p:["Research and an audit of the old site showed the problems quickly: oversized modules, products buried below the fold, and navigation that made people work for the basics. Category pages now open on product, product pages lead with fit and color, and the cart keeps the wishlist in reach."]},
      {media:[["s-b-dk-shop","Men’s Pants, the Original 874® product page, the cart and Men’s","full"]]},
      {k:"(Mobile-First)", h:"Swipe to shop.",
       p:["Most customers shop on their phones, so we designed for the phone first. Shoppable carousels fit more product on a small screen without endless scrolling, and tighter modules let the typeface and the photography carry the brand."]},
      {media:[["s-b-dk-mobile","Home, category, product, cart and Skateboarding on mobile","full"]]},
      {k:"(Categories)", h:"New lines, same modules.",
       p:["Best Sellers opened up to every category, the navigation got simpler, and the same modules stretched to Skateboarding, Scrubs and Dickies for Business without new templates."]},
      {media:[["s-b-dk-cats","Skateboarding, Scrubs, Dickies for Business and Home","full"]]},
      {k:"(Stories)", h:"Real people in the clothes.",
       p:["Dickies has stories worth telling, from racing builds to its skate team. Editorial and team pages run on the same Figma system as the store, so the team can publish a story as fast as a sale.",
          "The result is a faster, mobile-first storefront built to take a beating, just like the clothes."]},
      {media:[["s-b-dk-stories","Stories, Still in the Race, and skate team member Christian Henry","full"]]},
    ]''')
S['smartwool']=('s-b-sw-hero','Smartwool.com: home, activities, products and campaigns','''[
      {k:"(The Brief)", h:"A site that welcomes the overwhelmed.",
       p:["Working with VF Corporation and the Smartwool team, we took on the homepage. Smartwool makes great merino gear, and a lot of it. Socks alone come in dozens of cushion levels, heights and activities, and shoppers were landing on the site without knowing where to start.",
          "So we started over, from the structure up, backed by analytics, heuristic reviews and testing against competitors."]},
      {k:"(Directions)", h:"Two ways in, tested against each other.",
       p:["We explored more than one front door: a warm, campaign-led home built around the FP Movement collaboration, and a seasonal direction built around Cold Days Are Coming. Both led with the brand promise, and both put product within a scroll."]},
      {media:[["s-b-sw-dirs","The final home, and the earlier Cold Days direction","full"]]},
      {k:"(Architecture)", h:"A site map built on how people actually shop.",
       p:["People shop by what they are about to do, so activities became a front door: run, hike and bike, each with a clear path to men’s and women’s gear. Best sellers, collections and seasonal modules sit underneath, and category names were rewritten in plain language."]},
      {media:[["s-b-sw-arch","Shop by activity, best sellers, collections and seasonal gear","full"]]},
      {k:"(Discovery)", h:"A home for the stories the old site hid.",
       p:["Some of Smartwool’s best stories lived only in social campaigns. Campaign modules and product carousels gave collections like Plant-Based Dyes a place on the site, and a reason to explore."]},
      {media:[["s-b-sw-disc","Plant-Based Dyes and Gear Up for Dropping Temps","full"]]},
      {k:"(On Mobile)", h:"Everyone can find their way.",
       p:["On a phone, every module stacks into a clear path: activity, product, story, collection. The new homepage helps every customer, from first-time sock buyer to thru-hiker, find their way quickly, with the brand’s Go Far. Feel Good. promise front and center."]},
      {media:[["s-b-sw-mobile","Home, activities, what’s new, Plant-Based Dyes and collections on mobile","full"]]},
    ]''')
S['beacon']=('s-b-bc-hero','Beacon: home, components, the component gallery and color','''[
      {k:"(The Brief)", h:"Snackable wisdom for the busy designer.",
       p:["Beacon is Morgan Stanley’s customer-facing design system, supporting many digital products under one brand. The system worked. Adoption was the problem: designers were busy, the guidance was dense, and best practices were easy to skip.",
          "My job was to make the right thing the easy thing."]},
      {k:"(Getting Started)", h:"A front door designers want to open.",
       p:["The home leads with what’s new and one clear way in. Get Started hands designers the Figma and Sketch kits and a checklist, so a first visit ends with the right files open. Illustration did a lot of the work: every section got its own character, so the docs felt like a place worth visiting."]},
      {media:[["s-b-bc-start","The Beacon home and Get Started","full"]]},
      {k:"(Components)", h:"Answers in seconds, not chapters.",
       p:["The docs were reorganized into clear Foundations, Components and Patterns sections, each with design, development and accessibility tabs. Every component has its own page, and the gallery shows them all with their live states."]},
      {media:[["s-b-bc-comps","Components overview and the full gallery","full"]]},
      {k:"(Patterns)", h:"From parts to answers.",
       p:["Components solve small problems. Patterns solve the common big ones, like multi-step forms, empty states and errors, with guidance written in plain language. Synced Figma and Sketch kits meant what designers placed matched what engineering had built."]},
      {media:[["s-b-bc-pattern","Patterns: forms with inline validation","full"]]},
      {k:"(Foundations)", h:"A palette that can be bold and sensitive.",
       p:["Foundations cover type, color, elevation, grid and icons. Inter carries everything, and the palette leans on Morgan Stanley blues with a few brighter accents, each with its value and where it belongs.",
          "The color, typography, components and patterns pages were designed for this case study to complete the story, using the same components and illustrations as the original direction."]},
      {tokens:[["Light Blue","#187aba"],["Medium Blue","#005c90"],["Dark Blue","#002b51"],["Seafoam","#6fc8cb"],["Teal","#62e1e6"],["Pale Blue","#b9e8fe"],["Jade","#006600"],["Lilac","#675882"],["Action Blue","#0069b5"]], cap:"The Beacon palette"},
      {media:[["s-b-bc-found","Foundations: color and typography","full"]]},
    ]''')
